WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.636
Hey! Welcome to a new Project Kamp update!

00:00:02.636 --> 00:00:05.707
Last year, you saw us renovating this ruin.

00:00:05.707 --> 00:00:08.860
We took out the old roof and restored the brick walls.

00:00:09.754 --> 00:00:14.772
We built the wooden frame inside, 
with a new roof with reused tiles and insulated with cork..

00:00:14.772 --> 00:00:18.012
We then finished it with&nbsp;some nice-looking, local wood.

00:00:18.012 --> 00:00:21.204
This year, we've been using the top floor as an office.

00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:24.578
But, we still&nbsp;have two rooms in the bottom
 that we need to change up...

00:00:24.578 --> 00:00:30.068
In this video, we will renew one of them
 to&nbsp;convert it in a tiny cozy room.

00:00:30.068 --> 00:00:37.102
But, before we do that, we will help our neighbor right there 
to harvest&nbsp;some hay from our big grass patch.

00:00:40.531 --> 00:00:42.381
Our land has two big fields.

00:00:42.381 --> 00:00:47.700
Currently, grass is growing and&nbsp;once a year
our neighbor Jorge comes to cut it.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.831
We already harvested a bit before!

00:00:52.831 --> 00:00:55.380
We&nbsp;got some hay for our new compost toilet.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.700
Today, we are going to&nbsp;help him harvest the rest.

00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:36.131
For us, it's nice, because while&nbsp;doing this once a year...

00:01:54.792 --> 00:01:55.833
What about the kid?

00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:59.048
His name is Diogo, and he likes to help Jorge on the land.

00:01:59.048 --> 00:02:02.965
He would also like to be in YouTube, so there you go.

00:02:02.965 --> 00:02:06.840
This field was&nbsp;enough to fill more than six of these with hay!

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.168
First batch: done.

00:02:09.168 --> 00:02:10.380
Let's go for the second round!

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.460
It's nice that we can help the&nbsp;neighbors with simple things.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.200
Now, they have a good amount of hay,
that they&nbsp;can use to feed their animals during winter.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.520
Next, time to go a bit up on the&nbsp;land and
 see what we can do in the ruin!

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:29.484
So far, we just reinforced this area and installed two windows,
 but we left everything else quite&nbsp;raw.

00:03:29.484 --> 00:03:36.874
We used to use this space for storing our&nbsp;off-cuts of wood,
and a bit of other materials that needs to stay dry...

00:03:36.874 --> 00:03:41.123
But now, it's time to give another meaning to this space.

00:03:41.123 --> 00:03:43.962
The first step is to clean everything out.

00:03:43.962 --> 00:03:47.315
But, before we do that, let me&nbsp;introduce Ruben to you.

00:03:47.547 --> 00:03:48.712
He will be the artist.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.805
- Hey! My name is Ruben!

00:03:51.805 --> 00:03:55.088
I am from the&nbsp;Netherlands, and I am a carpenter and a builder.

00:03:55.088 --> 00:04:02.163
I came to Project Kamp, because I wanted to&nbsp;try community living,
working in nature, and learning about sustainable construction.

00:04:03.121 --> 00:04:06.046
I will&nbsp;be here to help renovating the bottom of the ruin.

00:04:06.046 --> 00:04:12.274
Back home, I'm used to building things&nbsp;in a certain way 
and fast and efficient, but not very sustainable...

00:04:12.274 --> 00:04:14.993
So, I wanted to see how&nbsp;things are done over here.

00:04:14.993 --> 00:04:16.020
Let's get started.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.040
- So, the first step is to take all&nbsp;these leftover wood out of the room.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.320
- You all right?

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.372
Yeah, all fine!

00:04:57.163 --> 00:05:01.603
After taking all the wood out,
we can see the state of the room.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.811
The floor is full of rubble...

00:05:04.811 --> 00:05:07.896
This was probably&nbsp;once a big slab of concrete.

00:05:07.896 --> 00:05:09.094
We don't know...

00:05:09.558 --> 00:05:12.896
As we want to make a&nbsp;new wooden floor, we will take it out.

00:05:12.896 --> 00:05:18.104
Removing this will hopefully also&nbsp;
make the floor of the room a bit higher.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:59.460
This piece of concrete was in good state, but&nbsp;we can't 
do anything with&nbsp;it, so we had to take it out.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.171
Okay, so, now we have cleared out all the bottom&nbsp;of the ruin.

00:06:10.171 --> 00:06:11.558
An amazing job.

00:06:11.558 --> 00:06:13.926
It's already looking way bigger now!

00:06:14.478 --> 00:06:17.548
So, we'll see what has been done inside.

00:06:17.548 --> 00:06:19.743
Now,&nbsp;we've already leveled the bottom.

00:06:19.743 --> 00:06:22.864
We see that it's way higher than we
 thought before, which&nbsp;is cool!

00:06:22.864 --> 00:06:29.704
We have a bit of a challenge here,
 because the walls are raw and different from&nbsp;each other.

00:06:29.704 --> 00:06:33.060
On one side, we have a huge granite wall!

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.553
We will probably patch this up with cement.

00:06:36.553 --> 00:06:41.207
You can find&nbsp;even some roof tiles tucked in it!

00:06:41.207 --> 00:06:44.435
A bit of a funny job they made here...

00:06:45.161 --> 00:06:51.246
On this side,&nbsp;we again have like a brick wall
 with some cement brought onto it.

00:06:51.246 --> 00:06:54.213
Some bricks, and some granite.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:55.338
It's a cool mix!

00:06:55.773 --> 00:06:57.585
We'll&nbsp;see what we do with it.

00:06:57.585 --> 00:07:01.620
In the front side, which is the south side of the house...

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:08.585
We have a bit of&nbsp;a similar situation, with the granite wall until here 
and everything up here is all bricks.

00:07:08.585 --> 00:07:10.179
We&nbsp;reinforced this before.

00:07:10.179 --> 00:07:15.708
In the roof, we find bricks with concrete lintels.

00:07:15.708 --> 00:07:20.280
On top, in the office,
there is a cement slab all over the floor.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.504
So, we know what is on top,
 because we already renewed the top part of this ruin.

00:07:26.781 --> 00:07:29.311
It's time to see what we're gonna do here.

00:07:29.892 --> 00:07:33.511
- So, we just want to make it to a nice usable room, you know?

00:07:33.511 --> 00:07:38.730
A cool place in summer, because the temperature over here&nbsp;
is a lot better here than the outside temperature...

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.435
We're gonna put some concrete posts all over&nbsp;the room.

00:07:42.435 --> 00:07:44.305
We will lay a wooden decked floor on it.

00:07:44.305 --> 00:07:50.284
Then, on top of that, we're gonna reuse an oak floor&nbsp;
from an old basketball gym from the nearby area.

00:07:50.284 --> 00:07:52.428
We're gonna have a lovely room over here!

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.174
- We are building&nbsp;this floor as a platform on top of the ground.

00:07:56.551 --> 00:08:03.009
We have put some concrete blocks to level certain points properly,
 that would help us level our floor.

00:08:06.029 --> 00:08:09.600
Then, we will make the wooden frame&nbsp;
that would support our platform.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.659
- Let's see how it then comes together.

00:08:14.922 --> 00:08:18.406
Our platform is made with an all sports cord flow.

00:08:18.406 --> 00:08:21.600
It is high quality wood&nbsp;and it was claimed in a nearby town.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.240
We are working very fast!

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.035
- Perfect!

00:09:02.766 --> 00:09:04.379
Floor: done!

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.173
So, as a next step, we're gonna build the door&nbsp;and a door frame.

00:09:09.173 --> 00:09:13.241
We're gonna cut it ourselves,
 and then we're gonna put in a&nbsp;40 millimeter door

00:09:13.241 --> 00:09:17.431
It'll be made out of leftover materials from other projects
 and it's gonna&nbsp;look really nice.

00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:21.181
Alright, so, we have a red cedar beam.

00:09:21.674 --> 00:09:26.182
We're going to cut it in half, and then
 we're going to make the door frame out of it.

00:09:26.182 --> 00:09:32.638
There's&nbsp;already some markings, so it's gonna be just cut in half.

00:09:32.638 --> 00:09:37.453
We then take this piece out for&nbsp;the door to fall into, 
and that's pretty much it!

00:10:12.481 --> 00:10:16.800
So, we have made a frame out of the cedar,&nbsp;
and now we're gonna look if it fits.

00:10:28.475 --> 00:10:30.695
- Does it fit, Ruben?

00:10:31.653 --> 00:10:34.547
We're working with the&nbsp;ruin, I guess?

00:10:34.866 --> 00:10:36.945
This is the best we can get it.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:46.837
We're gonna put granite slabs on the sides,
so there'll&nbsp;just be a fine line left of the window seal that you can see.

00:10:46.837 --> 00:10:51.000
Because of the rainfall, we're&nbsp;gonna have it after the wall actually.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:56.227
Just so there's no rain coming in
 and the walls are a little bit off...

00:10:56.227 --> 00:10:58.888
They're a bit plump.

00:10:58.888 --> 00:11:01.235
But, I guess we can make it work!

00:11:01.961 --> 00:11:03.251
- Cool!

00:11:03.251 --> 00:11:07.506
In order to attach the door, we first need to build the wall frames.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.866
What do you think about it?

00:11:52.866 --> 00:11:54.095
Is it strong enough?

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:55.488
- The house?

00:11:55.488 --> 00:11:59.217
Well, after I build this, it's a&nbsp;little bit stronger.

00:11:59.594 --> 00:12:02.564
But, I don't know if the house is very strong!

00:12:02.564 --> 00:12:07.352
I think the things&nbsp;we build over here however are very strong indeed.

00:12:18.786 --> 00:12:23.063
So, the largest part of the ruin is now ready.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:27.905
We have the door right here,
and I'm now gonna&nbsp;put the last touches on the door.

00:12:27.905 --> 00:12:34.620
So, I'm sanding it, putting in the hinges and lock,
and then it's ready for painting.

00:13:19.357 --> 00:13:23.340
We were looking for reused glass for putting in the door.

00:13:24.414 --> 00:13:29.616
We found two beautiful small ones 
that&nbsp;would fit exactly in the door.

00:13:29.616 --> 00:13:31.559
But, you know...

00:13:31.559 --> 00:13:34.073
As it is, you know that when we reuse materials...

00:13:34.073 --> 00:13:40.151
When we&nbsp;try to get them, we find out that the other one is broken.

00:13:40.151 --> 00:13:42.076
So, that's very&nbsp;sad...

00:13:43.324 --> 00:13:50.733
We looked somewhere else and we found two nice small ones,
 that we're gonna use next to the larger one.

00:13:50.733 --> 00:13:52.920
So, it won't work out in the end, I guess.

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:25.346
We only put two screws in, as you can see.

00:14:25.346 --> 00:14:31.035
So, the one in the middle, we're not putting it in 
 if we might need to move the hinge a little bit.

00:14:31.035 --> 00:14:35.596
We can loosen these two up, and then redirect the hinge.

00:14:35.596 --> 00:14:39.060
We will put in the last&nbsp;screw in 
and then screw these two down again.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.724
Well, it all fits perfectly!

00:15:09.508 --> 00:15:11.421
We have a&nbsp;lock now, so we can lock it.

00:15:11.973 --> 00:15:13.534
we still need to put the glass in

00:15:13.534 --> 00:15:19.719
The next step is going&nbsp;to be putting in the band,
just so that the glass can be stuck in it.

00:15:19.719 --> 00:15:24.002
So, we're just gonna put it in like&nbsp;that...

00:15:24.002 --> 00:15:31.380
We then try to cut it out with a knife.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.170
- Door: done!

00:15:50.762 --> 00:15:51.781
We're almost there!

00:15:52.275 --> 00:15:54.753
- So, we are all done with&nbsp;framing now.

00:15:54.753 --> 00:15:56.840
We've put up all the frames, as you can see.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:01.524
Now, we're gonna insulate it with&nbsp;50 millimeter cork insulation.

00:16:01.524 --> 00:16:08.114
We will do it all around the frame, so that the room will be nice and warm&nbsp;
during winter and nice and cold during the summer.

00:16:09.677 --> 00:16:12.977
- At the same time, Jaime is helping with the electricity.

00:16:27.476 --> 00:16:28.710
The room is almost ready.

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:31.177
We've put in the cork&nbsp;insulation.

00:16:31.177 --> 00:16:33.906
We did all of the wiring in the room.

00:16:33.906 --> 00:16:37.700
We've almost put all of the sockets in.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.628
Now, it's&nbsp;time for the next step!

00:16:39.628 --> 00:16:41.087
That will be the wall paneling.

00:16:41.087 --> 00:16:47.645
For that, we're gonna use a 22&nbsp;millimeters thick pine floorboard.

00:16:47.645 --> 00:16:50.789
It has a real nice quality and has a good&nbsp;finish on it!

00:16:50.789 --> 00:16:57.180
Also, it's a tongue and groove, so it fits nice into each other 
and it will&nbsp;give you room some extra strength overall.

00:18:03.892 --> 00:18:05.218
All right! All done!

00:18:05.682 --> 00:18:07.833
That was it for the walls!

00:18:07.833 --> 00:18:10.170
Now, we're gonna do the trim for the windows.

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:13.073
We're gonna do it with the same boards that&nbsp;we used for the walls.

00:18:13.073 --> 00:18:16.531
We put them upright like that, and make them a little longer.

00:18:16.531 --> 00:18:21.772
Also, some trim&nbsp;around it, so it all looks similar to the walls.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:33.761
Perfect!

00:18:34.313 --> 00:18:35.952
It fits like a glove!

00:18:51.709 --> 00:18:53.273
That was the last piece!

00:18:53.273 --> 00:18:55.377
The room&nbsp;is done now!

00:18:55.377 --> 00:18:56.820
All the sidings are up.

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:59.059
The floor is nicely in.

00:18:59.059 --> 00:19:00.837
And,&nbsp;yeah, that's it!

00:19:00.837 --> 00:19:02.212
My job is done!

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:08.575
So, that was it.

00:19:08.575 --> 00:19:09.576
it's all finished now!

00:19:09.576 --> 00:19:10.936
All the wood is in.

00:19:10.936 --> 00:19:17.608
It was a lot of work in a small time, but I really&nbsp;enjoyed 
working outside and being in nature all day working with wood.

00:19:17.608 --> 00:19:19.414
But, my&nbsp;time is up...

00:19:19.414 --> 00:19:21.283
It's time to hit the road again!

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:26.418
- This room is usable and very cozy.

00:19:27.009 --> 00:19:30.931
Even&nbsp;though it's not completely finished yet, we are already using it!

00:19:30.931 --> 00:19:38.894
From work, to taking a nap, doing&nbsp;yoga, having meetings,
and letting the bread dough rest.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:43.842
This is all possible, because the temperature&nbsp;inside is very stable.

00:19:43.842 --> 00:19:47.851
All thanks to the big granite wall 
and the new cork-isolated ones!

00:19:48.141 --> 00:19:50.257
But,&nbsp;there's still work to be done...

00:19:50.257 --> 00:19:54.636
Such as the ceiling, finishing the electric connections and&nbsp;lights.

00:19:54.636 --> 00:19:56.643
We will keep you updated on that!

00:19:57.107 --> 00:19:59.769
You often see us building new things on the land.

00:19:59.769 --> 00:20:04.157
But, a big&nbsp;part of our work is also repairing
 and maintaining these things.

00:20:04.157 --> 00:20:09.944
The conditions here are quite rough
and certain things tend to wear out, like gloves...

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:21.268
- So, as a part of our work, General Help is cleaning&nbsp;up the land.

00:20:21.268 --> 00:20:28.204
Very often, we end up having to pick through 
these brambly bushes as well as&nbsp;the very spiky kojo.

00:20:28.988 --> 00:20:33.156
This can, of course, lead to our gloves getting quite torn and ripped.

00:20:33.156 --> 00:20:38.073
So, I&nbsp;am on a mission to find a way to easily fix them for all of us.

00:20:38.392 --> 00:20:41.731
I've essentially divided out the&nbsp;gloves into two piles.

00:20:41.731 --> 00:20:44.249
On the left, we have the gloves that need to be repaired.

00:20:44.249 --> 00:20:48.126
Here&nbsp;on the right, you have gloves that are,
at this point, beyond repair.

00:20:48.126 --> 00:20:50.390
But, we are keeping them&nbsp;around, as you can see.

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:55.200
They still have some good material on them,
so we can use them&nbsp;as patches for the gloves that we CAN fix.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.930
When it comes to repairing these gloves by sewing...

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:07.570
Generally, what I like to do is doubling up the thread
 to make sure that every stitch&nbsp;is stronger.

00:21:08.238 --> 00:21:15.238
I will also stitch a knot every single time 
to ensure that, in case this gets&nbsp;cut while someone is working...

00:21:15.238 --> 00:21:21.469
All of the other stitches will still retain their hold on the&nbsp;glove,
and it can still be usable while they're&nbsp;working.

00:21:21.469 --> 00:21:25.001
For these repairs, I am using a very&nbsp;simple sewing needle.

00:21:25.001 --> 00:21:27.627
One which is, admittedly, a bit too small for leather.

00:21:28.585 --> 00:21:31.186
I am using this&nbsp;black nylon thread.

00:21:31.186 --> 00:21:36.610
I choose nylon, because I find that it is 
a lot stronger&nbsp;than normal thread.

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:38.957
And, just a pair of scissors.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:22:00.571
So, the positives here in sewing these gloves&nbsp;
is that it's quite a simple process.

00:22:00.571 --> 00:22:04.830
It can be done pretty much by anybody,
 and it's also&nbsp;generally, environmental friendly.

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.277
As of course, we're extending the lifespan of the gloves.

00:22:07.277 --> 00:22:10.846
We're also not using any chemicals or anything
 that might harm the earth around us.

00:22:10.846 --> 00:22:17.136
The only&nbsp;downside is that because the gloves are used
 in the manner that you've seen already...

00:22:17.136 --> 00:22:20.344
It means&nbsp;that these can tear again quite easily.

00:22:20.344 --> 00:22:22.815
Especially, at the point where they were repaired.

00:22:22.815 --> 00:22:27.847
In the&nbsp;case of some of these gloves, 
the holes are too big and the material has frayed so much.

00:22:27.847 --> 00:22:32.486
The sewing of it will really not help 
 as the material is already too weak...

00:22:32.486 --> 00:22:38.119
So, in these cases, we need&nbsp;to cut away
 the weak material and use a patch.

00:22:38.119 --> 00:22:40.994
For this, I discovered something in the&nbsp;workspace.

00:22:40.994 --> 00:22:42.058
Come with me.

00:22:42.819 --> 00:22:48.660
So, tucked away in the back of the workspace, we have a crate, which is&nbsp;full of all of our glues and different solvents.

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:54.289
In here, I found this product.

00:22:55.711 --> 00:22:58.937
It's a type&nbsp;of glue that you can use for all sorts of clothing materials.

00:22:58.937 --> 00:23:01.207
And, in general, it's very good for gluing leather.

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:05.501
So, we're going to be using this
 to patch up the gloves with the bigger holes.

00:23:05.501 --> 00:23:09.899
All we&nbsp;need is a bit of sandpaper
 and I'll show you why in a second.

00:23:09.899 --> 00:23:12.653
So, here we have everything that we need for this repair.

00:23:12.653 --> 00:23:14.690
We have the glove turned inside out.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:18.282
We have the little patch which I took&nbsp;from another glove.

00:23:18.282 --> 00:23:23.819
We have the glue, and we have some sanding paper or a sanding disk.

00:23:24.283 --> 00:23:30.423
Basically,&nbsp;what we need to do is to first sand both pieces
 in order to remove all of the dirt.

00:23:30.423 --> 00:23:33.764
To make sure&nbsp;that the material really adheres to the glue.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:46.082
We apply it to the patch.

00:23:47.853 --> 00:23:49.960
And, we&nbsp;apply it to the glove.

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:56.205
We let it sit for about five minutes.

00:23:56.205 --> 00:23:59.371
Only&nbsp;then I will put the patch onto the glove itself.

00:23:59.371 --> 00:24:05.517
Now, the challenge here is that when you 
try to&nbsp;put pressure on the patch...

00:24:05.517 --> 00:24:08.758
It can very easily glue to the other side of the glove...

00:24:08.758 --> 00:24:13.613
So,&nbsp;for that, I take this big stick 
and I actually insert it into the glove.

00:24:13.613 --> 00:24:16.615
To sort of replicate what&nbsp;it's like when a finger is in there.

00:24:16.615 --> 00:24:21.175
Then, it's easier to apply the patch 
and to put pressure&nbsp;while it dries.

00:24:21.175 --> 00:24:25.132
The plus side of using the glue is that 
it's much easier than sewing.

00:24:25.132 --> 00:24:28.080
It can&nbsp;create a much stronger bond as well in some cases.

00:24:29.373 --> 00:24:34.099
It is also easier when we're using patches,
because this material is quite thick.

00:24:34.779 --> 00:24:41.859
As you can see with this needle, I have already bent&nbsp;it almost 
completely in half, by just trying to sew through two layers.

00:24:41.859 --> 00:24:44.463
So,the glue makes it&nbsp;a lot easier in that regard.

00:24:44.463 --> 00:24:49.698
The downside is, of course, because it is so full of chemicals,
 it&nbsp;is a lot less environmental friendly...

00:24:49.698 --> 00:24:56.012
This has started to cure
 and now we can attach the two&nbsp;pieces just like that!

00:24:56.012 --> 00:24:59.801
We're ensuring that the glued contacts are touching.

00:24:59.801 --> 00:25:06.857
Then, we make sure to&nbsp;put some pressure for about a minute or so, 
and then we can leave it and let it cure on its own.

00:25:06.857 --> 00:25:09.330
That of itself is the whole process.

00:25:11.789 --> 00:25:12.984
- New gloves there, huh?

00:25:12.984 --> 00:25:13.808
Yeah!

00:25:13.808 --> 00:25:14.960
- Wow!

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:17.462
So, we've had some&nbsp;time to use 
some of the gloves that I repaired.

00:25:17.462 --> 00:25:23.571
As predicted with&nbsp;the sewing only,
 you can see that because of the work that we're doing...

00:25:23.571 --> 00:25:26.040
It becomes frayed&nbsp;and a lot of the knots start to break.

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:31.010
When it comes to using just the glue,
 although&nbsp;the patch is still holding...

00:25:31.010 --> 00:25:33.779
I'm seeing that the surrounding fabric is starting to fray.

00:25:34.279 --> 00:25:38.114
I&nbsp;have a feeling or a suspicion that it will not hold for very long

00:25:39.255 --> 00:25:42.807
So, what I did with one of&nbsp;these is that I used both.

00:25:42.807 --> 00:25:47.524
Essentially, I sewed a patch in,
 and then I applied glue over top of that.

00:25:47.524 --> 00:25:49.276
That seems to be holding up the best!

00:25:49.276 --> 00:25:51.633
So, this is what we're going to continue using.

00:25:51.633 --> 00:25:58.155
In the case&nbsp;of these more hardcore repairs,
where you have big holes and things that need to be filled.

00:26:03.668 --> 00:26:06.143
- So, it&nbsp;feels that these gloves are holding quite well!

00:26:06.143 --> 00:26:10.258
It's really nice to have almost brand new&nbsp;gloves.

00:26:10.258 --> 00:26:12.127
Thank you Alan for the repairs!

00:26:12.127 --> 00:26:13.520
It feel very solid!

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:14.707
They're really cool.

